[
  {
    "id": "big-o",
    "title": "Big-O Notation",
    "sceneType": "four-platform-growth (custom bar/block platforms)",
    "cards": [
      {
        "type": "explain"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "source": "first(a):\n    return a[0]\n\nsearch(a, x):\n    repeat: look at the middle, keep one half\n\ntotal(a):\n    for each item in a:\n        add it on\n\nany_pair(a):\n    for each item in a:\n        for each other item in a:\n            compare the two"
      },
      {
        "type": "facts",
        "title": "Steps taken"
      }
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "label": "Count steps, not seconds",
        "kind": "Concept",
        "body": [
          "How fast is an algorithm? Seconds depend on the machine. So instead we count <strong>steps</strong>, and ask how that count depends on the size of the input, <em>n</em>.",
          "Here are four kinds of algorithm working on 2 items. Each block is one step of work. They look much the same."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Notice",
          "text": "On small inputs almost anything is fast. The differences show when n grows."
        },
        "state": {
          "n": 2
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 2,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 4,
              "active": false
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 2 items."
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "Double the input",
        "kind": "Grow",
        "body": [
          "Give them 4 items instead of 2. One platform has not changed. One gained a single block. One doubled. One is four times the size.",
          "That reaction to growth is what Big-O describes."
        ],
        "state": {
          "n": 4
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 2,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 4,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 16,
              "active": false
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 4 items."
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "Double it again",
        "kind": "Grow",
        "body": [
          "8 items. The same thing happens again, each growing in its own way: not at all, by one, by double, by four times.",
          "Big-O names the <strong>shape of the growth</strong>, and ignores the details. 2n steps and n + 5 steps are both just O(n)."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Read it as",
          "text": "“O(n)” is “the work grows in step with n.”"
        },
        "state": {
          "n": 8
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 3,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 8,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 64,
              "active": false
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 8 items."
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "O(1): constant",
        "kind": "Constant",
        "body": [
          "The work does not depend on n at all. Reading an array cell by its index, pushing onto a stack: one step, for 8 items or 8 million.",
          "This is as good as it gets."
        ],
        "state": {
          "n": 8,
          "focus": "one"
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": true
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 3,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 8,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 64,
              "active": false
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 8 items."
        },
        "lines": [
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "O(log n): logarithmic",
        "kind": "Logarithmic",
        "body": [
          "Each step throws away half of what is left, as binary search does. Doubling the input adds just <strong>one</strong> more step.",
          "A million items take about 20 steps. A billion take about 30."
        ],
        "state": {
          "n": 8,
          "focus": "log"
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 3,
              "active": true
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 8,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 64,
              "active": false
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 8 items."
        },
        "lines": [
          4,
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "O(n): linear",
        "kind": "Linear",
        "body": [
          "Every item is looked at once: adding up a list, or searching one that is not sorted. Twice the input, twice the work.",
          "Often this is the best possible, because you cannot skip items you have never seen."
        ],
        "state": {
          "n": 8,
          "focus": "lin"
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 3,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 8,
              "active": true
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 64,
              "active": false
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 8 items."
        },
        "lines": [
          7,
          8,
          9
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "O(n²): quadratic",
        "kind": "Quadratic",
        "body": [
          "Every item is compared with every other: a loop inside a loop. Twice the input, <strong>four times</strong> the work.",
          "Fine for 8 items. For a million it is a trillion steps, and it is usually the first thing worth fixing."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "icon": "bolt",
          "text": "Much of this course is turning an O(n²) idea into O(n) or O(n log n)."
        },
        "state": {
          "n": 8,
          "focus": "quad"
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "O(1)",
              "detail": "constant",
              "value": 1,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(log n)",
              "detail": "logarithmic",
              "value": 3,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n)",
              "detail": "linear",
              "value": 8,
              "active": false
            },
            {
              "label": "O(n²)",
              "detail": "quadratic",
              "value": 64,
              "active": true
            }
          ],
          "note": "Steps for n = 8 items."
        },
        "lines": [
          11,
          12,
          13,
          14
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "stacks",
    "title": "Stacks",
    "sceneType": "stack-scene (input tray + vertical stack + output tray)",
    "sceneConfig": {
      "items": {
        "b10": "10",
        "b20": "20",
        "b30": "30",
        "b40": "40",
        "f1": "main()",
        "f2": "load()",
        "f3": "parse()"
      }
    },
    "cards": [
      {
        "type": "explain"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "source": "push(x):\n    put x on top\n\npop():\n    if the stack is empty: error\n    take the top item off\n    return it\n\ntop():\n    return the top item, leaving it there\n\nempty():\n    return whether nothing is left"
      }
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "label": "What is a stack?",
        "kind": "Concept",
        "body": [
          "A stack is a pile. You can add to the top and take from the top, and that is all.",
          "So the last item you put on is the first one you get back: <strong>LIFO</strong>, last in, first out."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Picture it",
          "text": "A pile of plates. Nobody pulls one out from the middle."
        },
        "state": {
          "stack": [
            "b10",
            "b20",
            "b30"
          ],
          "inbox": "b40"
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "Push",
        "kind": "Operation",
        "body": [
          "<strong>push(x)</strong> puts a new item on top. Here 40 leaves the input tray and lands on the pile.",
          "Nothing underneath has to move, so a push costs the same whether the stack holds three items or three million."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Cost",
          "icon": "bolt",
          "text": "O(1): one step, however tall the stack is."
        },
        "state": {
          "stack": [
            "b10",
            "b20",
            "b30",
            "b40"
          ],
          "op": "push"
        },
        "lines": [
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Top",
        "kind": "Operation",
        "body": [
          "<strong>top()</strong> tells you what is on top without taking it off. You will also see it called <em>peek</em>.",
          "The stack is exactly as it was afterwards."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Cost",
          "icon": "bolt",
          "text": "O(1): the top is always in the same place."
        },
        "state": {
          "stack": [
            "b10",
            "b20",
            "b30",
            "b40"
          ],
          "op": "top",
          "peek": true,
          "say": {
            "top": "top() → 40"
          }
        },
        "lines": [
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Pop",
        "kind": "Operation",
        "body": [
          "<strong>pop()</strong> removes the top item and hands it back. It is the only way to take anything out of a stack.",
          "40 was the last block in, so it is the first one out."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Watch out",
          "icon": "alert",
          "text": "Popping an empty stack is an error. Check empty() first."
        },
        "state": {
          "stack": [
            "b10",
            "b20",
            "b30"
          ],
          "out": [
            "b40"
          ],
          "op": "pop",
          "say": {
            "out": "pop() → 40"
          }
        },
        "lines": [
          4,
          5,
          6,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Last in, first out",
        "kind": "Idea",
        "body": [
          "Pop again and 30 comes out. The blocks went in as 10, 20, 30, 40 and they are leaving as 40, 30, …",
          "A stack always gives things back in the reverse of the order they arrived."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Notice",
          "text": "To reach 10 at the bottom, everything above it has to come off first."
        },
        "state": {
          "stack": [
            "b10",
            "b20"
          ],
          "out": [
            "b40",
            "b30"
          ],
          "op": "pop",
          "say": {
            "out": "pop() → 30"
          }
        },
        "lines": [
          4,
          5,
          6,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "Where you meet them",
        "kind": "In practice",
        "body": [
          "Each time a function calls another, the computer pushes a frame onto the <strong>call stack</strong>. When the function returns, its frame is popped and the caller carries on.",
          "Undo history and the browser’s Back button work the same way."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "By the way",
          "text": "“Stack overflow” is this stack running out of room, usually from recursion that never stops."
        },
        "state": {
          "stack": [
            "f1",
            "f2",
            "f3"
          ],
          "cascade": true
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "binary-trees",
    "title": "Binary Trees",
    "sceneType": "tree-scene (isometric nodes + parent-child links + optional depth levels)",
    "sceneConfig": {
      "labels": {
        "a": "A",
        "b": "B",
        "c": "C",
        "d": "D",
        "e": "E",
        "f": "F",
        "g": "G",
        "h": "H",
        "i": "I",
        "j": "J",
        "k": "K",
        "l": "L",
        "m": "M",
        "n": "N",
        "o": "O"
      },
      "spread": [
        192.5,
        96.25
      ],
      "room": [
        220,
        110,
        68.75
      ]
    },
    "cards": [
      {
        "type": "explain"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "source": "node:\n    value\n    left       // another node, or nothing\n    right      // another node, or nothing\n\ntree:\n    root       // the one node with no parent"
      },
      {
        "type": "facts",
        "title": "This tree"
      }
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "label": "One node, two children",
        "kind": "Concept",
        "body": [
          "A linked-list node points at one other node. Give a node <strong>two</strong> pointers, <em>left</em> and <em>right</em>, and the list becomes a tree.",
          "A is the <strong>root</strong>: the one node nothing points at. B and C are its <strong>children</strong>, and A is their <strong>parent</strong>."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Picture it",
          "text": "A family tree drawn with the oldest ancestor at the top."
        },
        "state": {
          "tree": {
            "a": "",
            "b": "L",
            "c": "R"
          },
          "say": {
            "at": "a",
            "text": "the root"
          }
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Nodes",
              "value": 3
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves",
              "detail": "no children",
              "value": 2
            },
            {
              "label": "Height",
              "detail": "longest way down",
              "value": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "lines": [
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "It keeps branching",
        "kind": "Concept",
        "body": [
          "Each child can have two children of its own, and so on down.",
          "A node with no children is a <strong>leaf</strong>. D, E, F and G are the leaves here: every way down from the root ends at one."
        ],
        "state": {
          "tree": {
            "a": "",
            "b": "L",
            "c": "R",
            "d": "LL",
            "e": "LR",
            "f": "RL",
            "g": "RR"
          },
          "lit": [
            "d",
            "e",
            "f",
            "g"
          ],
          "say": {
            "at": "d",
            "text": "a leaf"
          }
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Nodes",
              "value": 7
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves",
              "detail": "no children",
              "value": 4
            },
            {
              "label": "Height",
              "detail": "longest way down",
              "value": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "Depth and height",
        "kind": "Measure",
        "body": [
          "A node’s <strong>depth</strong> is how many branches lie between it and the root. The root is at depth 0, its children at depth 1.",
          "The tree’s <strong>height</strong> is the depth of its deepest leaf: here, 2. Most of what a tree costs to use depends on its height."
        ],
        "state": {
          "tree": {
            "a": "",
            "b": "L",
            "c": "R",
            "d": "LL",
            "e": "LR",
            "f": "RL",
            "g": "RR"
          },
          "levels": true
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Nodes",
              "value": 7
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves",
              "detail": "no children",
              "value": 4
            },
            {
              "label": "Height",
              "detail": "longest way down",
              "value": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "Trees inside trees",
        "kind": "Concept",
        "body": [
          "Look only at B and everything below it. That is a tree too, with B as its root: a <strong>subtree</strong>.",
          "Every node is the root of its own subtree. This is why so much tree code is recursive: do the job for a node, then for its left subtree, then its right."
        ],
        "state": {
          "tree": {
            "a": "",
            "b": "L",
            "c": "R",
            "d": "LL",
            "e": "LR",
            "f": "RL",
            "g": "RR"
          },
          "lit": [
            "b",
            "d",
            "e"
          ],
          "dim": [
            "a",
            "c",
            "f",
            "g"
          ],
          "say": {
            "at": "b",
            "text": "a subtree"
          }
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Nodes",
              "value": 3
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves",
              "detail": "no children",
              "value": 2
            },
            {
              "label": "Height",
              "detail": "longest way down",
              "value": 1
            }
          ],
          "note": "Counting only the subtree under B."
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "How much fits",
        "kind": "Growth",
        "body": [
          "Every level can hold twice as many nodes as the one above: 1, 2, 4, 8. One more level roughly doubles the whole tree.",
          "So a full tree of height 3 holds 15 nodes, and one of height 20 holds over two million. A lot of data is only a few steps from the root."
        ],
        "note": {
          "title": "Remember",
          "icon": "bolt",
          "text": "n nodes can fit in a height of about log₂ n. That is where tree speed comes from."
        },
        "state": {
          "tree": {
            "a": "",
            "b": "L",
            "c": "R",
            "d": "LL",
            "e": "LR",
            "f": "RL",
            "g": "RR",
            "h": "LLL",
            "i": "LLR",
            "j": "LRL",
            "k": "LRR",
            "l": "RLL",
            "m": "RLR",
            "n": "RRL",
            "o": "RRR"
          },
          "spread": [
            220,
            110,
            68.75
          ],
          "levels": true
        },
        "facts": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Nodes",
              "value": 15
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves",
              "detail": "no children",
              "value": 8
            },
            {
              "label": "Height",
              "detail": "longest way down",
              "value": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "label": "Not always tidy",
        "kind": "Watch out",
        "body": [
          "Nothing says a binary tree must be full. “Binary” only promises <em>at most</em> two children.",
          "This one has five nodes and is as tall as the fifteen-node tree was. Taken to the extreme, with one child everywhere, a tree is just a linked list."
        ],
        "state": {
          "tree": {
